a historic Hollywood filming site, is being rebuilt nearly eight years after the 2018 Woolsey Fire destroyed most of its buildings and sets. The National Park Service is spending $26 million on the project, with new fire-safe structures being built in the footprints of historic Paramount-era buildings. The ranch is expected to reopen in 2027.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1927-paramount-bought-a-2700-acre-california-ranch-and-filmed-more-than-160-movies-there-after-wildfire-destroyed-almost-everything-in-2018-a-26-million-rebuild-is-nearly-complete/articleshow/133964321.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1927-paramount-bought-a-2700-acre-california-ranch-and-filmed-more-than-160-movies-there-after-wildfire-destroyed-almost-everything-in-2018-a-26-million-rebuild-is-nearly-complete/articleshow/133964321.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T17:50:17+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133966059,imgsize-141286.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Retired 40-metre dredger sunk off Adelaide to create new artificial reef]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133963114.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[South Australia has sunk the 40-metre MV Andrew Wilson six kilometres off Brighton to create an artificial reef. The vessel now rests 20 metres underwater and forms part of a $165 million algal-bloom recovery effort. Fishing is banned within 200 metres of the wreck for 12 months to help local fish stocks recover.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/south-australia-sank-a-40-metre-dredger-six-kilometres-off-adelaide-the-ship-now-rests-20-metres-underwater-as-a-new-reef-in-a-165-million-algal-bloom-recovery-plan/articleshow/133963114.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/south-australia-sank-a-40-metre-dredger-six-kilometres-off-adelaide-the-ship-now-rests-20-metres-underwater-as-a-new-reef-in-a-165-million-algal-bloom-recovery-plan/articleshow/133963114.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T17:30:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133964092,imgsize-77718.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Indian family deported from Canada after asylum claim fails: 'When Canada needed Harish, he was essential']]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133968725.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/indian-family-deported-from-canada-after-asylum-claim-fails-when-canada-needed-harish-he-was-essential/articleshow/133968725.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/indian-family-deported-from-canada-after-asylum-claim-fails-when-canada-needed-harish-he-was-essential/articleshow/133968725.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T17:47:27+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133968799,imgsize-78404.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Meet Franklin Howley-Dumit Serulle, whose online insect photo helped reveal a new hoverfly]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133960389.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[An amateur naturalist's photograph on iNaturalist helped scientists identify a new hoverfly species. Researchers later found a specimen in Valle Nuevo National Park, confirming the discovery. This new species, Monoceromyia ndidiae, was formally described after laboratory analysis. The discovery highlights how amateur observations contribute to understanding biodiversity. This finding earned the Swiss Systematics Society’s New Species of the Year 2026 title.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/wildlife/meet-franklin-howley-dumit-serulle-the-amateur-naturalist-whose-online-insect-photo-helped-reveal-a-new-hoverfly-species/articleshow/133960389.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/wildlife/meet-franklin-howley-dumit-serulle-the-amateur-naturalist-whose-online-insect-photo-helped-reveal-a-new-hoverfly-species/articleshow/133960389.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T15:50:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133960734,imgsize-84624.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Meet the scientist who helped reveal Costa Rica’s mysterious ghost shark]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133964276.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/wildlife/meet-naidely-valeria-vidaurre-quesada-the-scientist-who-helped-uncover-a-ghost-shark-species-hiding-in-costa-ricas-dark-waters/articleshow/133964276.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/wildlife/meet-naidely-valeria-vidaurre-quesada-the-scientist-who-helped-uncover-a-ghost-shark-species-hiding-in-costa-ricas-dark-waters/articleshow/133964276.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T16:40:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133964553,imgsize-959030.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[17 waste materials tested to make stronger, lower-carbon cement building products]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133960018.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A James Dyson Award project explores using waste for construction materials.  Recycled aggregates like eggshells and glass replace conventional materials in cement.  This approach reduces reliance on newly extracted natural resources.  The project aims to create thinner, stronger cement boards and tiles.  Further testing will assess durability and recycling potential for these innovative products.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/discovery/human-hair-receipts-fishing-nets-and-eggshells-were-among-17-waste-materials-tested-to-create-stronger-lower-carbon-cement-building-products/articleshow/133960018.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/discovery/human-hair-receipts-fishing-nets-and-eggshells-were-among-17-waste-materials-tested-to-create-stronger-lower-carbon-cement-building-products/articleshow/133960018.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T15:30:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133960182,imgsize-188122.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[‘Unfounded accusations’: China rejects US claims of 'malicious' AI distillation by its firms]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133964569.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/unfounded-accusations-china-rejects-us-claims-of-malicious-ai-distillation-by-its-firms/articleshow/133964569.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/unfounded-accusations-china-rejects-us-claims-of-malicious-ai-distillation-by-its-firms/articleshow/133964569.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T16:18:10+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Aashish Vashistha</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133965179,imgsize-78467.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Alaska saw 30,000 ship arrivals in 11 years; their hulls created a hidden invasive species risk]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133961664.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Researchers found that nearly 30,000 commercial vessel arrivals brought 430 sq km of submerged surface area to Alaska between 2012 and 2022. The study says this surface can carry marine organisms through biofouling. Passenger vessels accounted for more than half of the total area, while warming waters and increased Arctic shipping could raise the risk of non-indigenous species reaching Alaska.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/nature/from-2012-to-2022-nearly-30000-commercial-vessel-arrivals-brought-430-sq-km-of-submerged-hull-surface-to-alaska-biofouling-habitat-grew-50-increasing-invasive-species-risk/articleshow/133961664.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/nature/from-2012-to-2022-nearly-30000-commercial-vessel-arrivals-brought-430-sq-km-of-submerged-hull-surface-to-alaska-biofouling-habitat-grew-50-increasing-invasive-species-risk/articleshow/133961664.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-09T16:30:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133962428,imgsize-146641.cms"/></item></channel></rss>